(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-10-prevention-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 10: Prevention Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial of screening chest x-rays and sputum cytology for lung cancer, approximately 9,000 men were randomized to screening for 6 years or a control group. After 20 years, the lung cancer mortality was the same in both groups (4.4/1,000 person-years in the screened group and 3.9/1,000 person-years in the control group). However, the median survival for patients diagnosed with lung cancer was 1.3 years in the screened group and 0.9 years in the control group. Also, screening found more lung cancer—206 cancers were diagnosed in the screened group and 160 in the control group.\n\nWhat bias is the best explanation for the improved survival in the face of no improvement in the mortality rate in this study?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Survival bias","Compliance bias","Length-time bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. See answer for 10.4. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.5"},{"q":"Researchers in Denmark looked at the incidence of breast cancer over 3 decades in areas of the country where mammogram screening was introduced compared to locations where screening was not offered. Small invasive cancers (<2 cm) were found more frequently in the screened group, but the incidence of large invasive cancers (>2 cm) was similar for the screened and unscreened groups. These findings remained after controlling for age differences. Which of the following is true?","choices":["If lead-time bias is present, both cancer survival rates and cancer mortality rates will be lower in the screening group.","Many of the smaller tumors detected may not have become clinically significant","Overdiagnosis is not present because the cancers found were invasive.","The study does not account for possible changes in the cancer incidence over time."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The findings could be due to overdiagnosis, where the smaller tumors detected would not have become clinically significant. With lead-time bias, cancers are diagnosed earlier (thus increasing the apparent survival with cancer), even if cancer mortality rates are unchanged. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.7"},{"q":"A review compared the performance of HPV testing versus cytology (pap smears) for cervical cancer screening. Compared to cytology, HPV testing had better detection of precancerous lesions that would be treated to prevent subsequent cancers. However, it also had a higher false-positive rate. Which of the following is incorrect?","choices":["HPV is more sensitive than cytology for screening.","HPV is less specific than cytology for screening.","More frequent testing may improve the sensitivity of cytology.","The positive predictive value is higher for HPV than cytology."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Compared to cytology, a single HPV test is more sensitive (it detects a higher percentage of lesions) but less specific (for patients without lesions, there are fewer true negatives and more false positives). HPV testing compared to cytology therefore has a better negative predictive value but lower positive predictive value for a given prevalence. Sensitivity of either test can be improved and specificity worsened with more frequent testing. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.8"},{"q":"When assessing a new vaccine, which of the following is least important:","choices":["Efficacy in preventing the disease","Safety of the vaccine","Danger of the disease","Cost of giving the vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Several important questions are to be considered when assessing a new vaccine, including: (i) Has the vaccine been shown to be efficacious (did it work under ideal circumstances when everyone in the intervention group received the vaccine and no one in a comparable control group did) and was it effective (under everyday circumstances, which people in the intervention group did not receive the vaccine)? (ii) Is the vaccine safe? (iii) Is the burden of suffering of the condition the vaccine protects against important enough to consider a preventive measure?, and (iv) Is it cost-effective? Cost of the vaccine is only one component of an analysis of cost-effectiveness. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.9"},{"q":"When the same test is used in diagnostic and screening situations, which of the following statements is most likely correct?","choices":["The sensitivity and specificity will likely be the same in both situations.","The positive predictive value will be higher in a screening situation.","Overdiagnosis is equally likely in both situations.","Disease prevalence will be higher in the diagnostic situation."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Disease prevalence is lower in presumably well people (screening) than in symptomatic patients (diagnosis). As a result, positive predictive value will be lower in screening. Because screening is aimed at picking up early disease, the sensitivity of most tests is lower in screening than in patients with more advanced disease. Overdiagnosis is less likely in diagnostic situations when symptomatic patients have more late-stage disease. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.10"},{"q":"For a cost-effectiveness analysis of a preventive activity, which kinds of costs should be included?","choices":["Medical costs, such as those associated with delivering the preventive intervention","All medical costs, including diagnostic follow-up of positive tests and treatment for persons diagnosed with disease, with and without the preventive activity","Indirect costs, such as loss of income due to time off from work, among patients receiving the prevention and those who develop the disease","Indirect costs for both patients and care givers","All of the above"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Cost-effectiveness should be estimated in a way that captures all costs of the preventive activity and all the costs associated with diagnosis and treatment, wherever they occur, to determine the costs of the preventive activity from a societal perspective. All costs that occur when prevention is not done are subtracted from all costs when prevention is done to estimate the cost for a given health effect. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.13"}]};
